WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.400
SOC 2 readiness depends on control owners and evidence.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:07.460
A control owner is responsible for understanding what the control requires,

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.440
how often it must operate,

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.820
what systems or teams it covers, what evidence proves operation,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.120
and what to do when the control does

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.100
not operate as expected.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.720
Without ownership, controls become promises without a reliable operating path.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:27.420
Evidence should be accurate, complete, timely, and traceable.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.720
It should show what happened, when it happened, who performed or reviewed it,

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.700
which system or population was covered, and how exceptions were handled.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.440
Evidence does not need to be beautiful, but it needs to be trustworthy.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.980
A messy but authentic ticket trail is better than a polished document that does not

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.560
reflect reality.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.100
Common evidence includes access review records, onboarding and offboarding tickets,

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.240
change approvals, pull request records, deployment tickets,

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.500
policy acknowledgments, security training completion, risk assessments, vendor reviews,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.960
backup test results, monitoring alerts, incident response records,

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.260
vulnerability remediation records, configuration screenshots,

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.880
and meeting notes where decisions are documented.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.900
The exact evidence depends on the control, scope, auditor, and review period.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:20.040
Type II readiness requires recurring evidence collection.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.100
If a control operates monthly, evidence should be collected monthly.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.800
If a control operates when a change occurs, evidence should be tied to changes.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.260
If a control operates during incidents,

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.680
the team should preserve incident records when incidents happen.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:39.620
Waiting until the end of the period and trying to reconstruct months of activity creates

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.120
risk and stress.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.820
Engineering, security, IT, HR, operations,

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.320
and vendor-management teams often contribute evidence.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:52.100
HR may own onboarding and offboarding records.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.880
Engineering may own change management evidence.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.020
Security may own monitoring, incidents, vulnerability management,

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.640
and access review coordination. Operations may own backup tests.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.500
Vendor management may own third-party reviews.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.240
SOC 2 is cross-functional because real controls are cross-functional.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.700
The ethical rule is simple: do not fabricate or misrepresent evidence.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.080
Do not create fake logs, hide failed controls, backdate approvals,

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.380
or remove exceptions to look cleaner.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.800
If a gap exists, document it, evaluate impact, remediate it,

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:30.700
and improve the process. Honest evidence supports trust.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.560
Misleading evidence defeats the purpose of the report.
